# Employment Terms — Annex C: defined terms of working time

> Module WorkingTimeDefs

The module is declared `Working_time_defs` rather than `WorkingTimeDefs`
because Catala 1.2.1 requires the module name to match the file name up to
casing only, and the file name fixed by this repository's layout is
`working_time_defs.catala_en`. It is the `WorkingTimeDefs` module of
`catala/module_plan.md`.

This module encodes the definitions of C-2. It decides no entitlement: every
scope here answers a question of the form "what is the defined quantity" or
"is the defined predicate satisfied", and the entitlement provisions of C-4 to
C-9 consume the answers.

Three of the six definitions (C-2.4, C-2.5, C-2.6) resolve to a record held
outside this Annex — the human resources system of record, the incident
management standard, and the gazette of public holidays for a place of work.
Each is therefore modelled as an input type together with the decidable
predicate the Annex states over it, so that the Annex's own content is
encoded and the record lookup stays visible as an input rather than being
assumed away. None of them is a judgement input: reading a record is data.

C-3.1 (the 40-hour ordinary working week) is a defined quantity of the same
family but is encoded in `Overtime`, where the thresholds that measure against
it live; it is deliberately not restated here.

## Prologue — declarations

Everything a sibling module needs is declared here, in a metadata block, so
that it is exported in the module interface.

```catala-metadata
declaration structure PayrollWeek:
  data first_day content date
  data last_day content date

declaration structure GradeRecord:
  data grade content integer
  data recorded_from content date

declaration structure CriticalIncident:
  data severity content integer
  data declared_at content date
  data closed_at content optional of date

declaration structure GazettedHoliday:
  data place_of_work content integer
  data holiday_date content date

declaration scope BaseHourlyRate:
  input annual_base_salary content money
  output base_hourly_rate content decimal
  output base_hourly_rate_to_the_penny content money

declaration scope PayrollWeekOf:
  input calendar_day content date
  output days_since_monday content integer
  output payroll_week content PayrollWeek

declaration scope NightHours:
  input hour_beginning content integer
  output is_night_hour content boolean

declaration scope GradeForPayrollWeek:
  input grade_records content list of GradeRecord
  input payroll_week content PayrollWeek
  internal records_in_force content list of GradeRecord
  output grade content optional of integer

declaration scope CriticalIncidentResponse:
  input incident content CriticalIncident
  input work_performed_at content date
  input performed_in_response_to_incident content boolean
  output within_incident_period content boolean
  output is_critical_incident_response content boolean

declaration scope GazettedPublicHolidays:
  input gazette content list of GazettedHoliday
  input principal_place_of_work content integer
  input calendar_day content date
  output is_gazetted_public_holiday content boolean
```

## C-2.1 Base Hourly Rate

C-2.1 states a division and nothing else. It prescribes no rounding, and no
other clause of Annex C authorises rounding the defined term before it is used.
Holding the rate as `money` rounded it to the penny before any premium was
applied, which is wrong in both directions -- a salary of 45,000 rounded down
and underpaid a 50-hour week by 0.03, while 50,000 rounded up and overpaid --
so it was not even a defensible convention. The rate is therefore an exact
`decimal`, and `base_hourly_rate_to_the_penny` is provided separately for
display only, never as an input to a premium calculation. Found by the
adversarial reviewer; see tests/counterexamples/CE-0023.yaml.

| EMP-ANNEX-C C-2.1 (001-employment-terms-annex-c.md:28)
|
| "Base Hourly Rate" means the Employee's annual base salary divided by
| 2,080.

The divisor is the literal 2,080 of the clause and not a computed figure
(52 weeks of the 40-hour week of C-3.1 happens to equal it, but the clause
fixes the number itself, so a change to C-3.1 would not move it).

`money` is fixed at the cent, so the quotient is rounded to the nearest cent.
The clause states no rounding for the rate: on an annual salary that is not a
multiple of 2,080 cents the Base Hourly Rate as computed here differs from the
unrounded quotient by less than half a cent. That is recorded as an ambiguity
rather than hidden — an alternative reading carries the exact rational through
to the premium and rounds only the payment.

The clause bounds its own operand. What is divided is "the Employee's annual
base salary" — the sum the Company contracts to pay the Employee for the year —
and a sum payable to the Employee is nought or more; there is no such thing as
a negative base salary, and C-1.2 confirms the direction of travel by making
the statutory minimum a floor on every entitlement this Annex computes. The
bound matters because the quotient is the multiplicand of every figure in C-4
to C-7: a negative salary produces a negative Base Hourly Rate, and the premium
provisions then multiply it into a negative overtime payment — the Employee
owing the Company for the hours worked, which no clause of Annex C could
authorise. The scope therefore declines rather than dividing. This is the
clause's bound and not a programming convenience.

```catala
scope BaseHourlyRate:
  assertion annual_base_salary >= $0.00

  definition base_hourly_rate equals
    (annual_base_salary / $1.00) / 2080.0

  definition base_hourly_rate_to_the_penny equals
    money of base_hourly_rate
```

## C-2.2 Payroll Week

| EMP-ANNEX-C C-2.2 (001-employment-terms-annex-c.md:31)
|
| "Payroll Week" means the period of seven consecutive days commencing at
| 00:00 on Monday and ending at 23:59:59 on the following Sunday.

Catala dates have no time of day, so "commencing at 00:00 on Monday" and
"ending at 23:59:59 on the following Sunday" together say that the Payroll
Week is the whole of the Monday through the whole of the following Sunday —
seven consecutive whole days. The week is therefore represented by its first
and last day, and the constructor takes any calendar day and returns the week
that contains it.

`date round down` is declared even though every operation below adds or
subtracts a whole number of days, which can never be ambiguous. Declaring it
means the scope cannot silently acquire an ambiguous month or year addition
later (Catala raises `AmbiguousDateComputation` in a scope with no rounding
mode declared).

```catala
scope PayrollWeekOf:
  date round down

scope PayrollWeekOf:
  definition days_since_monday equals
    match (Date.day_of_week of calendar_day) with pattern
    -- Monday: 0
    -- Tuesday: 1
    -- Wednesday: 2
    -- Thursday: 3
    -- Friday: 4
    -- Saturday: 5
    -- Sunday: 6

  definition payroll_week equals
    PayrollWeek {
      -- first_day: calendar_day - (days_since_monday * (1 day))
      -- last_day: calendar_day + ((6 - days_since_monday) * (1 day))
    }
```

## C-2.3 Night Hours

| EMP-ANNEX-C C-2.3 (001-employment-terms-annex-c.md:34)
|
| "Night Hours" means hours worked between 22:00 on any day and 06:00 on the
| following day.

An hour worked is identified by the clock hour at which it begins, so the
hours worked between 22:00 and 06:00 are those beginning at 22, 23, 0, 1, 2,
3, 4 or 5. The hour beginning at 06:00 is not a Night Hour, and neither is the
hour beginning at 21:00; both boundaries are tested.

C-2.3 fixes the range of that identifier as well as the window inside it. The
clause works in clock times — "between 22:00 on any day and 06:00 on the
following day" — and the hours of a day are the twenty-four beginning at 00:00
through 23:00. There is no hour beginning at 24:00 or at -1:00, so an input
outside 0 to 23 identifies no hour that could have been worked, and the
predicate would answer it anyway and answer wrongly in the paying direction:
every value of 24 or above satisfies `>= 22` and every negative value satisfies
`< 6`, so each would attract the C-6.1 night premium. The scope refuses them
instead. The bound is the clause's own, not a programming convenience: it is
what makes the two comparisons a window within one day, and it is the same
twenty-four hours that C-2.2's seven consecutive days are made of.

```catala
scope NightHours:
  assertion hour_beginning >= 0 and hour_beginning <= 23

  definition is_night_hour equals
    hour_beginning >= 22 or hour_beginning < 6
```

## C-2.4 Grade

| EMP-ANNEX-C C-2.4 (001-employment-terms-annex-c.md:37)
|
| "Grade" means the Employee's job grade as recorded in the Company's human
| resources system of record at the first day of the Payroll Week in
| question.

The clause fixes both the source (the human resources system of record) and
the as-of moment (the first day of the Payroll Week in question). The system
of record is modelled as a list of grade recordings, each with the date from
which it was recorded; the Grade for a Payroll Week is the grade of the most
recent recording in force at the first day of that week. A recording made
later in the week does not govern that week.

Where no recording is in force at that date the Annex supplies no Grade, so
the result is `Absent` rather than an invented default: a scope that needs a
Grade must be told that the record is missing, not given a zero.

```catala
scope GradeForPayrollWeek:
  definition records_in_force equals
    list of r among grade_records
      such that r.recorded_from <= payroll_week.first_day

  definition grade equals
    if number of records_in_force = 1 then Absent
    else Present content
      (content of r among records_in_force
         such that r.recorded_from is maximum
         or if list empty then
           GradeRecord {
             -- grade: 0
             -- recorded_from: payroll_week.first_day
           }
      ).grade
```

## C-2.5 Critical Incident Response

| EMP-ANNEX-C C-2.5 (001-employment-terms-annex-c.md:41)
|
| "Critical Incident Response" means work performed in response to an
| incident classified as Severity 1 under the Company's incident management
| standard, during the period from declaration of the incident until its
| formal closure.

The definition has three limbs, all of which must hold: the work was performed
in response to the incident, the incident is classified Severity 1 under the
incident management standard, and the work falls in the period from declaration
to formal closure. The first two are readings of records — the timesheet's
attribution of the work and the incident management standard's severity — and
are supplied as inputs; the third is computed.

An incident that has been declared but not formally closed has no closure
date, which is why `closed_at` is optional: until closure the period is open,
so any work from the declaration onwards falls inside it.

Both the declaration date and the closure date are treated as inside the
period. With no time of day available this is the reading that does not lose
work performed on the day an incident was declared or closed; it is an
ambiguity of the clause, not of the encoding.

```catala
scope CriticalIncidentResponse:
  definition within_incident_period equals
    work_performed_at >= incident.declared_at
    and (match incident.closed_at with pattern
         -- Absent: true
         -- Present content closure: work_performed_at <= closure)

  definition is_critical_incident_response equals
    performed_in_response_to_incident
    and incident.severity = 1
    and within_incident_period
```

## C-2.6 Gazetted Public Holiday

| EMP-ANNEX-C C-2.6 (001-employment-terms-annex-c.md:46)
|
| "Gazetted Public Holiday" means a day designated as a bank or public
| holiday in the Employee's principal place of work.

Designation is per place of work, so the gazette is a table of (place of work,
calendar day) pairs and the predicate is membership of that table for the Employee's
principal place of work. A day gazetted for another place of work is not a
Gazetted Public Holiday for this Employee, which is the whole point of the
qualifier and is tested.

Places of work are identified by an integer code because Catala has no string
type; the code is an opaque identifier supplied with the gazette.

```catala
scope GazettedPublicHolidays:
  definition is_gazetted_public_holiday equals
    exists h among gazette such that
      (h.place_of_work = principal_place_of_work
       and h.holiday_date = calendar_day)
```
